course: dictionary
lesson: 13832
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it: they are its context. «He kept jabbing me with his pencil.» Jabbing means poking or pushing something sharp quickly and hard. The word «kept» tells you it happened again and again.
explain: Jab can also be a boxing word. «The boxer threw three quick jabs in the first round.» Boxers hit each other with their fists, so a jab here is a quick, short punch. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence? Then try it in place of the word and see if it still makes sense.
explain: The clues can also point to a meaning that is not about hurting anyone at all. «Tom had his flu jab at the doctor's.» Flu and doctor's show that a jab here is an injection, which is a vaccination. In the UK this is informal. «Her jabbing remarks made Sam go quiet.» Remarks are words, so jabbing means making sharp, unkind comments.
fact: Jabbing means poking or pushing something sharp or pointed quickly and hard.
fact: In boxing, a jab is a quick, short, straight punch.
fact: A jab (UK informal) is an injection or vaccination, such as «the flu jab».
fact: Figuratively, jabbing can mean making sharp, unkind remarks at someone.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «He kept jabbing me with his pencil.» What is he doing? | Singing a song | Drawing a picture on my arm | Hugging me gently | *Poking me quickly and hard with the sharp end
exercise: «The boxer threw three quick jabs in the first round.» What does jabs mean here? | Warm cups of tea | Long, loud shouts | *Quick, short straight punches | Slow, gentle pats
exercise: «Tom had his flu jab at the doctor's.» What is a jab in this sentence? | *An injection or vaccination | A game played on ice | A kind of sandwich | A sharp pencil
test: «Her jabbing remarks made Sam go quiet.» What does jabbing suggest here? | Loud singing | Happy, funny jokes | *Sharp, unkind remarks | Soft, sleepy talk
test: Which meaning fits «the flu jab»? | A boxing punch | *A vaccination, an injection | A kind of hat | A quick poke with a finger
exam: «Stop jabbing me, Ella!» What does Ella want? | Them to come closer | *Them to stop poking her | Them to stop reading | Them to stop singing
